          }
        ],
        "text": "According to John H. Blume (Petitioner), according to John H. Blume (Petitioner), the case concerns the admissibility of third\u2011party guilt evidence when the prosecution has presented strong forensic evidence."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "John H. Blume (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2494e2ba9e268c93709bd109fb1cd21b051450ef91dba6bbcdcffab1af6d3cd5",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2494e2ba9e26-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to John H. Blume (Petitioner), according to John H. Blume (Petitioner), the South Carolina Supreme Court previously limited a defendant\u2019s ability to create reasonable doubt by presenting third\u2011party guilt evidence, first in State v. Gay (2001) and now in this case."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Blume",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2494e2ba9e268c93709bd109fb1cd21b051450ef91dba6bbcdcffab1af6d3cd5",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2494e2ba9e26-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Blume, other states allow third\u2011party guilt evidence if it creates a reasonable inference of innocence."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Blume",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2494e2ba9e268c93709bd109fb1cd21b051450ef91dba6bbcdcffab1af6d3cd5",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2494e2ba9e26-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Blume, even when the State\u2019s case is strong, the jury should be allowed to consider alibi witnesses."
      }
    ],
